{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":595896,"ns":0,"title":"Chowla–Mordell theorem","extract":"In mathematics, the Chowla–Mordell theorem says that for a Dirichlet character modulo an odd prime, if the argument of its Gaussian sum is a root of unity, then the character must be quadratic. It is a result in number theory determining cases where a Gauss sum is the square root of a prime number, multiplied by a root of unity.","description":"When a Gauss sum is the square root of a prime number, multiplied by a root of unity","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}